## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical AWS knowledge of private content delivery, CloudFront with OAI, and Lambda@Edge integration, plus a tangible test plan.

## Key Concepts
- CloudFront Origin Access Identity (OAI)
- S3 bucket policies restricting GetObject to OAI only
- TLS enforcement and viewer protocol policy
- Lambda@Edge for header augmentation
- End-to-end testing of CDN delivery vs direct S3 access

## Code Example
```javascript
'use strict';
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const request = event.Records[0].cf.request;
  const tenantId = (request.headers['x-tenant-id'] && request.headers['x-tenant-id'][0].value) || 'unknown';
  request.headers['X-Tenant-Id'] = [{ key: 'X-Tenant-Id', value: tenantId }];
  return request;
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate the CloudFront OAI and keys securely?
- How would you test the bucket policy with simulated access from non-CloudFront principals?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of encryption, key management, cross-account access, and operational rotation at scale with compliance demands.

## Key Concepts

- Envelope encryption
- KMS CMKs and data keys
- Key rotation strategies
- Background re-encryption of existing data
- IAM/bucket policies per tenant
- Auditability with CloudTrail

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: rotate tenant data keys
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const kms = new AWS.KMS();
async function rotateTenantDataKey(cmkArn) {
  const { CiphertextBlob, Plaintext } = await kms.generateDataKey({ KeyId: cmkArn, KeySpec: 'AES_256' }).promise();
  // Store CiphertextBlob in tenant metadata; use Plaintext to encrypt new data in memory (do not persist plaintext)
}
```

